\f1\fs22 Hayley called Sarah down to the parlour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Two Elvi appeared in jumpsuits emblazoned with white and gold dragons. Sarah and Dr Logan followed. Something in their closeness and the swing of their arms suggested they\'92d been holding hands until just before entering. Dr Logan met Hayley with an arrested smile, unable to hide the crimson blooming in her cheeks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah!\'94 Alexander effused, rushing to greet her, but the dragon Elvi blocked him, squaring their aggressive chests.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come, sit down, Airborne Jacobson.\'94 Hayley gestured at the two-seater couch.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If you could give us the room, Dr Logan... you too, lieutenant. I\'92d like to interview Airborne Jacobson alone, please.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A chorus of objections erupted:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah: \'93Kathy stays.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy: \'93I\'92m not leaving her.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander: \'93I remind you, I\'92m the executive producer of\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander truncated his statement of pre-eminent privilege as the door swung open. At the threshold stood Eloise Shakti, in a business suit whose valuation\'97the last time a Big Two PricewaterDoublefudge auditor lived to write up the paperwork\'97could have bought not just every jumpsuit and poncho on the estate, but the actual estate itself, three and a half gunships, a respectable corner of the local black market, and enough fried chicken to cater every 
\f2\i Twilight Zone
\f1\i0  marathon through to next Kentucky Derby day.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mother!\'94 Alexander blurted, voice suddenly up an octave.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Across the room, Hayley squared up to the new arrival. \'93Excuse me, I\'92m Detective Inspector Hayley Lanning\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Detective Inspector/
\f2\i Executive Producer
\f1\i0  Hayley Lanning,\'94 Alexander inserted, baritone restored.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'97and I\'92m here on an official investigation,\'94 Hayley continued.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise sized up Hayley in an instant, then ignored her. \'93Alexander, go wait in the car.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But, mother...\'94 Alexander whined.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise hit him with that look, the one all mothers have pre-installed. Alexander\'92s mouth dropped; finding no support, he slunk off. \'93Yes, mother.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After Alexander clicked the door behind him, Eloise reluctantly acknowledged the detective in the room. \'93Your interview will have to wait, Detective Inspector/Executive Producer Hayley Lanning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m not one of the lieutenant\'92s sideshow underlings,\'94 Hayley replied, jaw set.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise admired the woman\'92s courage. \'93Oh, I can see that. It\'92s just that\'85 well, my presence is something of a powder keg wherever I go, so I\'92d imagine any intelligent woman would rather me gone as expeditiously as possible. If you\'92ll allow me a quiet word with Sarah, I\'92ll be on my way.  And, as a public service, I\'92ll take my son with me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley calculated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do we have an arrangement?\'94 Eloise pressed. \'93I promise I won\'92t be long. Then you can continue your... investigation.\'94 She waggled her fingers, as if telling children to run along.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley huffed. \'93Five minutes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you. Now, if you\'92ll excuse us\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She\'92s not leaving,\'94 Kathy said, matter-of-fact.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise smiled as graciously as a snake. \'93And you are?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m Dr Logan, MD PhD.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Taking good care of Sarah, I expect. Is she at any immediate risk, health-wise?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She isn\'92t.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then, please wait outside, doctor. And detective.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They\'92re both staying,\'94 Sarah asserted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise considered, eyes flicking to a corner of the ceiling. \'93Very well. Detective, go sit with the others, so I can see you all. Sarah, dismiss your Elvi guards.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ll stay with the Holy Momma,\'94 said an Elvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i Holy Momma?
\f1\i0 \'92 echoed Eloise, amused. \'91Indeed. Do recall you who loaned you the tanks in your front yard? If anyone is defending the Holy Momma here, it\'92s me. It would be a shame if, say, the original buyer of those tanks should suddenly require them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s okay,\'94 Sarah assured the Elvi, grateful be free of them for a time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi withdrew, humming under their breath. The last one mimed a karate chop as he left.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pulled Alexander\'92s vacated chair up close to Sarah sitting between Kathy and Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No entourage this time?\'94 Sarah challenged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise shrugged, with an effortless confidence. \'93You\'92ve seen my demonstrations. I assumed no more were necessary.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah swallowed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good. Now\'97Sarah. What are we to do with Alexander?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Poor, little Alexander,\'94 Eloise mused, eyes out of focus in decades-old memory. \'93He had a good heart, you know. Wanted the best for everyone, such a sweet child. But, his appetite for the best for himself first... clouded his altruism. Did you know? When he dreamed up 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 , the plan was to target Airborne crimes. I think there\'92s always a bit of Alexander fighting for his Cloudborne mother. He set his sights on corporate fraudsters and rogue traders.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, that was never going to happen,\'94 Hayley said dryly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise gifted Hayley the glance of glance reserved for junior staffers who mispronounce 
\f2\i synergy
\f1\i0  at shareholder meetings. \'93It took him two seasons to twig that the research team was feeding him fakes with hired actors. He tried sacking them, but they threatened to leak it was his idea. So they pitched the current format instead.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But that\'92s fake too,\'94 Kathy said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is it?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They stared at Hayley, as if she had answers. \'93Don\'92t look at me. The Public Affairs Bureau\'97which is practically your son\'92s personal fiefdom\'97is sealed off from us common grunts.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Celebrity will do that to a person,\'94 Eloise said. \'93Once the show caught on\'97once Alexander got famous\'97he forgot his ideals.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s a psychopath,\'94 Hayley ruled. \'93Delusional.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s no psychopath,\'94 Eloise insisted, with a residue of disappointment. \'93He feels deeply... inherited that from his father,  may he rest without tax. Lysander was shrewd, if a bit dull and lacking drive. Classic, conservative Airborne, drawers full of compliance medals and privilege citations. What he ever saw in me...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A warlord?\'94 Hayley offered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m an arms dealer, not a warlord,\'94 Eloise corrected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Forgive me for muddling the difference.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I never seek war, merely the profit from the threat of it. A warlord blows things up; an arms dealer only eyes the next product cycle, the next obsolescence upgrade.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley bristled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s straightened. \'93The last thing I need is a real war that exposes how dysfunctional most the kit is. Far better that arms are stockpiled, forgotten, then replaced by new ones\'97which, I provide in plentiful supply. The old fireworks need never be lit.\'94  Some of the starch leeched from Eloise\'92s posture. \'91Now. I was talking about my dear, late husband, Lysander: he defied them all, you know\'85 marrying me, a Cloudborne. And it cost him dearly. Of course, we lived comfortably, but never at the level he could have\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise appeared to wipe a tear from her eye and even Hayley was almost convinced. \'93I genuinely believed I was good enough for him,\'94 she said softly. \'93By the time I was twenty, I owned three houses and two apartments. Within two years, I was running my own Accommodation Directorate: still I wanted more. Like a proper Airborne, the chase became the reward. I competed ferociously, crushed rivals underfoot.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Stabbed them with her heels
\f1\i0 , Sarah thought as she glanced at Eloise\'92s exquisite shoes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I would have been the first Cloudborne founder to go public,\'94\'97Eloise\'92s gaze hardened\'97\'93until the Stock Exchange filed an injunction with Moral Compliance. Then came the lowball offers to buy me out, at a tenth of par, I as forced to sell. And I swore, never again, I took what liquidity I had salvaged and invested it\'85 underground, shall we say?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And that\'92s how a loving wife becomes an arms dealer,\'94 Hayley said, deadpan. \'93Happens every day.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let silence spread, then fixed Hayley with an obsidian glare. \'93I want to like you, Detective Inspector Hayley Lanning. As a woman of grit, you have my deepest respect. So please, stop being tiresome.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley blanched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What I came for, is to discuss my son,\'94 Eloise told Sarah. \'93If your companions can\'92t keep quiet\'85\'92 Eloise let the threat hang.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What about Alexander?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise stood, pacing the room, toying with flowers, muffins, a risqu\'e9-wrapped toy. \'93Alexander is clearly taken with you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Obsessed, more like,\'94 Kathy interposed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let that pass, her patience barely masked. She addressed Hayley: \'93You called Alexander delusional. But is it delusion when every dream comes true?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93By your hand,\'94 Sarah noted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise nodded, sombre. \'93But who can fault a mother for wanting the best for her son?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And tax everyone else,\'94 Hayley shot back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise slumped and, just for a moment, looked genuinely drained. \'93We sent Alexander to all the best schools\'97I mean 
\f2\i all
\f1\i0  of them. Every school expelled him, eventually. Once, he got a report card: across the board, every teacher wrote, 
\f2\i Monkeys retain information better
\f1\i0 . We knew he had issues, but this...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s professional negligence,\'94 Kathy said, scandalised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Poor Alexander,\'94 Eloise sighed, sympathy bleeding at last. \'93We did everything for him: curated toys, activities, playgroups, everything optimised for a future corporate raider. None of it stuck. We got him help\'85 In the end, they all said the same thing: W
\f2\i hat can anyone expect when his mother is
\f1\i0 \'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Cloudborne!\'94 Kathy completed the sentence with Eloise. Kathy fumed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, years later, I might have firebombed a medical centre or two.\'94 The silence became as thick as concrete, then Eloise seemed to wilt, face pinking. \'93I know Alexander is... not bright. That\'92s on me. I suppose a Cloudborne can\'92t escape genetics.  Alexander will never be truly accepted in Airborne society.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise\'92s appalling demonstration of vulnerability lasted not a second longer than she intended. With a subtle performance of increasing resolve, she regathered her grit. Her posture straightened, as if her bones had grown a little. Her gaze drifted across the three women on the couch\'97Kathy\'92s protective hand still on Sarah\'92s thigh, Hayley bristling in defence of them both, Sarah caught between them like someone genuinely cared for rather than merely useful.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know what strikes me about you three? You actually... support each other.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, I\'92ve just met them,\'94 Hayley said, blushing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That's okay.\'94 Kathy reached around Sarah and gave Hayley the best hug she could. \'93We can be friends,\'94 she said and giggled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ve seen plenty of alliances, partnerships, arrangements,\'94 Eloise continued, \'93and I can read chemistry from across the nerve agent lab. There\'92s something about you three.\'94 Her voice carried an unfamiliar wistfulness. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She paused, seeming to catch herself in the sentiment, then appeared to make a decision.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alexander grew up thinking love was another kind of transaction.\'94 She looked at Sarah, her expression softening in a way that made even Hayley shift uncomfortably. \'93You know what I regret most about my marriage to Lysander?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question hung in the air as if she hadn\'92t meant to ask it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That it ended too soon for me to discover if we could have been... just happy. Not strategic, not advantageous\'97just two people who chose each other every day.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The silence stretched. Eloise seemed to remember where she was, but instead of retreating, she leaned forward slightly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alexander has that same look when he talks about you. Not the calculating one\'97the other one. The one that says he thinks you might choose him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She straightened, her familiar composure returning, though something vulnerable lingered underneath.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"But first, Sarah\'97who's the father of your child?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stammered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is it Anthony Chantara?\'94 Eloise pressed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No!\'94 Sarah protested, stung.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s not Alexander, is it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? No! Never\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank tax,\'94 Eloise said, genuine relief glinting. \'93Then whose?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t see why that\'92s your business,\'94 Kathy said, defensive, as she pulled Sarah close. Hayley, nerves jangling, shrank off towards the couch arm, fussing with her hair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise dismissed Kathy with a blink. \'93Are you happy here, Sarah? In this little fortress, with all your Elvi?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I, ah...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m here to get you out. But first, answer two questions to my satisfaction and I\'92ll set you free.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And why would you do that?\'94 Kathy demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise arrested an eye-roll. To Sarah: \'93I\'92ll explain everything\'97after. Now, please...\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93His name is Clinton,\'94 Kathy blurted, patting Sarah\'92s thigh. \'93Clinton Dawkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise didn\'92t bother with Kathy. \'93And who is Clinton Dawkins?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s... an old friend,\'94 Sarah said, picking up the thread.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He is Airborne, I hope?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh\'97yes! He\'92s an evolutionary biologist. Top of his field. The elite.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m not sure I\'92m familiar with that. How did you two meet?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sorted through her library of plausible half-truths. She felt seen-through. \'93A karaoke bar.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A karaoke bar?\'94 Eloise repeated, incredulous.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We bonded over Elvi music,\'94 Sarah explained. \'93Clint loves Elvi music.\'94\
